export default "Drip and Floriva take different approaches to private period tracking. Drip is open source, so anyone can confirm it is private. Floriva is cross-platform and polished, so people actually use it. Both reduce company-held data by keeping core records local.\n\n## The Core Difference\n\nDrip is a free, open-source period tracker built by community contributors. Its source code is public. Anyone can read it, audit it, or fork it. You do not take the developer's word for it. You read the code.\n\nFloriva is a closed-source app funded by subscriptions. It has a clean UI, optional encrypted sync, regular updates, and a dedicated development team. Its privacy model for core tracking is local-first and no-account. But you trust the company's claims rather than verifying them yourself.\n\n## Data Architecture\n\n**Drip** stores all data in local device storage. No network calls for user data. No account creation. No analytics SDKs. Because the code is open, you can confirm this by reading the source. The app contains no server communication code for user data.\n\n**Floriva** stores core records on-device. Optional cross-device sync uses end-to-end encryption so Floriva cannot read the synced data. No account is required for core tracking. Since Floriva is closed source, these claims rest on the company's representations rather than auditable code.\n\nBoth reduce the company subpoena target. Drip has no readable central records. Floriva has no readable central cycle database. The difference is how you verify that.\n\n## Features\n\nDrip is intentionally minimal. It tracks periods, temperature, mucus, and a small set of symptoms. The interface is functional rather than designed. Charts are basic. Predictions use standard calendar and symptom-thermal methods.\n\nFloriva offers more: detailed symptom logging, pattern analysis, cycle predictions, and a designed interface with visual data presentation. The experience gap between the two is real. Floriva is a consumer product. Drip is a privacy tool.\n\nNeither app includes social features, community forums, or health content libraries. Both focus on tracking.\n\nFor users who want a [zero-knowledge period tracker](/resources/guides/what-is-zero-knowledge-period-tracker), both qualify. The question is whether you want minimal-and-verifiable or polished-and-trusted.\n\n## Pricing\n\n**Drip:** Free. No premium tier. Community-maintained with volunteer development. The risk: volunteer projects can stall. If the maintainers move on, updates stop. The app would keep working but would not receive bug fixes or OS updates.\n\n**Floriva:** Paid subscription, no free tier. Subscription funds ongoing development. Paying users expect updates, so the model creates a direct reason to keep building.\n\n## Who Should Choose Floriva vs Drip\n\n**Choose Drip if:** you are on Android, you value open-source verification over polish, you want a free tracker with no corporate entity behind it, or you can audit code and want to verify privacy claims yourself.\n\n**Choose Floriva if:** you use an iPhone (Drip is not available), you want optional encrypted sync, you prefer a cleaner interface, or you want a dedicated team maintaining the product. Check the [best private period tracker apps](/resources/best/best-private-period-tracker-apps) for a full comparison.\n\n## The Privacy Bottom Line\n\nDrip offers something Floriva cannot: proof. Open-source code is the highest standard of privacy verification. You do not need to trust anyone. You read the code.\n\nFloriva offers something Drip cannot: iOS support and an app that does not require privacy expertise to use. Most people will not audit source code. They want an app that works on their phone and does not store their data on someone else's server.\n\nBoth are privacy-first trackers. The choice depends on whether you value verifiability or usability, and whether you own an iPhone.";
